1. Paste in the random string from wiremask <enter>
2. Check the EIP register and copy the hex value into the pattern￾generator website. It will then tell you the offset.
3. The offset is how many "A's" we need to get to the EIP
4. Now begins the python coding…
5. Buffer variable is the number of "A's" needed to reach the EIP
register (the offset)
6. EIP variable is what we want to write to the EIP.
7. We put four B's so we can make sure we landed exactly on target
8. The C's in buf are to make sure we got it right
9. When it crashes, our EIP should be BBBB. If there is an A, our
offset is too long. If there is a C, our offset is too short.
4. (localflow.py)
#!/usr/bin/python
buffer='\x41' * 62 #<bufferoffset>
EIP='\x42' * 4
buf='\x43' * 10
print(buffer+EIP+buf)
